---
import CalculatorLayout from '@/layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import NpvCalculator from '@/components/calculators/NpvCalculator';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is NPV?',
    a: 'Net present value is the value today of all a project’s future cash flows, discounted at your required rate of return, minus what you invest. A positive NPV means the project earns more than that rate.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I calculate NPV?',
    a: 'Divide each year’s cash flow by (1 + rate) raised to the year number, add them up, and subtract the initial investment. For $10,000 invested with cash flows of $3,000, $4,000, $4,000 and $3,000 at 10%, NPV = $1,087.36.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What discount rate should I use?',
    a: 'Use the return you could get elsewhere for similar risk, often your cost of capital or a target return. Riskier projects deserve higher rates. Try a few rates to see how sensitive the result is.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between NPV and IRR?',
    a: 'NPV gives an amount of value at a chosen rate; IRR gives the rate at which NPV is zero. When comparing projects of different sizes, NPV is usually the better guide because it measures how much value is created.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the profitability index?',
    a: 'The present value of the future cash flows divided by the initial investment. Above 1 means a positive NPV. In the example, 11,087.36 ÷ 10,000 = 1.109.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  slug="npv-calculator"
  title="NPV Calculator – Net Present Value of Cash Flows"
  description="Free NPV calculator. Find the net present value of an investment from yearly cash flows and a discount rate, with present values, IRR and profitability index."
  intro="Find the net present value of an investment from its yearly cash flows and your required return, with the present value of each year, the IRR and the profitability index."
  faqs={faqs}
  updated="2026-09-27"
>
  <NpvCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="learn">
    <h2>How to use the NPV calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Enter the initial investment</strong> (money spent today).</li>
      <li><strong>Set the discount rate:</strong> the return you require.</li>
      <li><strong>Enter the cash flow for each year.</strong> Add or remove years as needed.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>NPV formula</h2>
    <div class="formula">NPV = −Initial investment + CF₁ ÷ (1 + r) + CF₂ ÷ (1 + r)² + … + CFₙ ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ</div>

    <h3>Worked example</h3>
    <p>You invest <strong>$10,000</strong> and expect $3,000, $4,000, $4,000 and $3,000 over four years. At a <strong>10%</strong> discount rate:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Cash flow</th><th>Present value</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>$3,000</td><td>$2,727.27</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>$4,000</td><td>$3,305.79</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td>$4,000</td><td>$3,005.26</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td>$3,000</td><td>$2,049.04</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The present values add up to $11,087.36, so <strong>NPV = $1,087.36</strong>: the project is worth taking at a 10% required return.</p>

    <h2>How the discount rate changes NPV</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Discount rate</th><th>NPV</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>5%</td><td>$2,408.72</td></tr>
        <tr><td>8%</td><td>$1,587.55</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10%</td><td>$1,087.36</td></tr>
        <tr><td>12%</td><td>$621.02</td></tr>
        <tr><td>15%</td><td>−$21.41</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>NPV turns negative just below 15%, which is where the IRR is (14.90%). Find it exactly with the <a href="/irr-calculator">IRR calculator</a>.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
